Operators and Functions

1. arr.push()
=> Adds element at the end of the array and return the new length of the array. Mutates the original array
2. arr.pop()
=> Removes the last element of the array and return it. Mutates the original array
3. arr.shift()
=> Removes the first element of the array and returns the removed element.Takes more time than pop
as first element is removed and rest elements are shifted to the begining.
4. arr.unshift()
=>unshift() adds one or more elements to the beginning of an array and returns the new length. It also mutatates the original array
It adds the elements in the order you pass them.
5. arr.slice(start, end)
=> slice() is used to extract a portion of an array without modifying the original array.Start index is is included but end is not
=> If just one argument is provided like slice(2), it means start from 2nd index and go till the end (end is included)
=> If no arg is provided like slice(), entire array is sliced to a new array. This way is used to make shallow copies of the array
=> Array indexex starts from 0 and goes from start to end. But it can be represent from end to start using negative indexes
where end is represented by -1 and the other element like -2 -3 so on till the start
so slice(-2) means slice the array from second last element to the end
6. arr.splice()
splice() is used to add, remove, or replace elements in an array.
array.splice(start, deleteCount, item1, item2, ...)
deleteCount = 0  → ADD
deleteCount > 0  → REMOVE
deleteCount > 0 + new items → REPLACE
splice() returns an array containing the removed elements.
7. arr.concat()
concat() is used to combine arrays or values and return a new array.
=> Concate also creates a shallow copy
8. indexOf()
indexOf() searches for a value in an array and returns its first index.
indexOf() returns -1 when the element isn't found.
*indexOf(searchElement, fromIndex) You can also specify from which index search should start
9 lastIndexOf()
It is similar to indexOf(), but it searches from the end of the array and returns the last occurrence of a value.
array.lastIndexOf(searchElement, fromIndex) The search will start from fromIndex and go from right to left
10. includes()
includes() checks whether an array contains a particular value.
array.includes(value, fromIndex) It can also take starting index as argument
includes() can correctly detect NaN, whereas indexOf() does not.
const arr = [1, 2, NaN];

console.log(arr.includes(NaN));
// true

console.log(arr.indexOf(NaN));
// -1
11. find()
find() returns the first element that satisfies a condition.
if nothing matches it returns undefined
12. findIndex()
findIndex() → returns the index of the matching element
if nothing matches it returns -1
13. some()
it checks whether at least one element in the array satisfies a condition.
it returns a boolean value.
empty array will return false since there is no element that satisfy the condition
14. every() 
it checks whether ALL elements satisfy a condition.
For empty array every() returns true since there is no element that violates the condition.
15. filter() 
it creates a new array containing all elements that satisfy a condition.
filter() returns an empty array, not undefined if nothing matches
16. map()
map() transforms every element and returns a new array.
17 forEach()
forEach() executes a function once for each element in the array.
The forEach() method itself does not modify the array.But you can modify the array inside the callback.
18 reduce()
reduce() processes all array elements and accumulates them into a single final value.
const nums = [10, 20, 30, 40];
intialVlaue = 0
const sum = nums.reduce((acc, current) => {
  return acc + current;
}, initialValue);

console.log(sum);
// 100
acc     → accumulator
current → current array element
initialValue -> Initial value of the accumulator
The accumulator carries the result from one iteration to the next.
If the initial value is not provide reduce takes the first element as acc and starts from 2nd value.

19 sort()
sort() converts elements to strings and sorts them lexicographically (dictionary-style).
const arr = [10, 2, 30, 5];

arr.sort();

console.log(arr);//[10, 2, 30, 5]
"10" → starts with "1"
"2"  → starts with "2"
"30" → starts with "3"
"5"  → starts with "5"
For numeric sorting use comparator functions inside sort()
arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);

JavaScript uses the returned number to decide the order of a and b.

The rule
negative → a comes BEFORE b
positive → a comes AFTER b
zero     → keep their relative order
sort() mutates the original array

20. reverse()
reverse() reverses the order of elements in place.
It also mutates the original array

21. join() 
converts the elements of an array into a string, with a separator between them.
join() does not mutate the original array.

22. flat() is used to remove nested array levels.
It removes one level of nesting by default. but you can mention the level as arg like arr.flat(2).
arr.flat(infinity) will flatten all the nested elements
returns a new array and not mutate the original one

23. flatMap() 
combines map() + flat(1) into one operation.

24 at() 
lets you access an element by its index.
Negative index means at starts looking form right to left.
If index is out of bounds then at returns undefined

